&lt;li>Test&lt;/li>
&lt;/ol>
&lt;p>If you try to list the Nodes, you&amp;rsquo;ll get an error similar to the following one:&lt;/p>
&lt;div class="highlight">&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;">&lt;code class="language-bash" data-lang="bash">&lt;span style="display:flex;">&lt;span>$ kubectl get no
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span style="display:flex;">&lt;span>
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;span style="display:flex;">&lt;span>The connection to the server 192.168.64.17:6443 was refused - did you specify the right host or port?
&lt;/span>&lt;/span>&lt;/code>&lt;/pre>&lt;/div>&lt;ol start="2">
&lt;li>Identification&lt;/li>
&lt;/ol>
&lt;p>As kubectl targets the API Server, first make sure this one is running fine.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>On the controlplane Node, check the APIServer&amp;rsquo;s logs in &lt;em>/var/log/syslog&lt;/em>. You should see that the API Server cannot start correctly:&lt;/p></description></item></channel></rss>